Team,

Canonical direction for Roast.pet / FunnyLabs / Pog:

DO NOT build a bespoke Canva/Product Creator. The point is to reuse
existing editor / preview / print infrastructure because this layer
will change quickly and we only need enough to validate the product.

CORE PRODUCT FLOW

Muse / ChatGPT / website / Etsy are all adapters over the same Roast.pet MC=
P.

User:
=E2=80=9CMake Dad a birthday card from Buster. He=E2=80=99s 50 and still th=
inks he=E2=80=99s
good at golf.=E2=80=9D

System:
1. resolve / create Pog identity
2. build RoastRequest
3. generate card design state
4. render preview
5. show preview inline in chat
6. user says things like:
   - =E2=80=9Cuse the other Buster photo=E2=80=9D
   - =E2=80=9Cmake the headline meaner=E2=80=9D
   - =E2=80=9Cmove him left=E2=80=9D
   - =E2=80=9Cmake it less savage=E2=80=9D
7. patch the design state
8. re-render preview
9. on approval, render print files
10. fulfil through Prodigi
11. QR on card opens Roast.pet/Pog reveal
12. same Pog asset is reused in FreakTown video / AR

IMPORTANT ARCHITECTURE

Do not make the chat itself be the editor.

The universal primitive is a rendered preview image:
- preview-flat.png
- preview-mockup.png
- print-ready PDF/PNG

The chat only needs to display those inline and expose a small set of
tools. If the user wants fine manual control, open the real editor.

The same backend should support:
- Muse
- ChatGPT
- Roast.pet website
- Etsy orders
- future commerce agents

So:

Muse / ChatGPT / Etsy / website
          =E2=86=93
      Roast.pet MCP
          =E2=86=93
 canonical design JSON
          =E2=86=93
 editor / renderer
          =E2=86=93
 preview + print assets
          =E2=86=93
 Prodigi / other fulfilment engines

MCP TOOL SURFACE

Keep it boring and finite:

create_card(template_id, pog_id, recipient, occasion)
set_pet_asset(pog_id)
set_headline(text)
set_subheadline(text)
set_teaser(text)
set_signoff(text)
set_background(style)
replace_image(asset_id)
move_layer(layer_id, x, y)
render_preview()
render_print_files()
approve_card()
order_physical()

Muse should be able to stay inside our MCP from request =E2=86=92 preview =
=E2=86=92
revision =E2=86=92 fulfilment. The user does not need to leave chat unless
they explicitly want the full editor.

EXISTING TECH TO REUSE

1. Polotno / artelo-demo
https://github.com/polotno-project/artelo-demo

This is extremely close to what we need:
- design editor
- canvas locked to physical product dimensions
- 300-DPI PNG export
- Cloudflare Worker
- R2 upload
- print-on-demand API handoff
- order state

Steal the architecture. Swap its print provider for Prodigi. Do not
recreate this pipeline.

2. Polotno Studio / agent-native design approach

Use the same conceptual model:
design JSON
=E2=86=95
human editor / agent tools
=E2=86=93
render/export

The valuable primitive is that agents and humans operate on the same
document state.

3. Fabric.js
https://github.com/fabricjs/fabric.js

Use this if we want fully owned/open-source rendering instead of
depending on a commercial SDK.

Useful editor references:
https://github.com/ajpgtech/design-editor
https://github.com/bharathreddyza/react-design-editor

Do not fork their whole UI. Reuse patterns / components / serialization.

4. Konva
https://github.com/konvajs/konva

Alternative canvas stack if Fabric becomes awkward.

RECOMMENDED V1

React + SVG/Fabric.js + JSON card schema + Cloudflare Worker + R2 + Prodigi=
.

Do NOT start with a full drag/drop editor.

Our card templates should be constrained.

Example design object:

{
  "template": "roast-club-v1",
  "front": {
    "pet_asset_id": "pog_buster",
    "headline": "JAMES IS 50.",
    "subheadline": "BUSTER HAS NOTES."
  },
  "inside_left": {
    "teaser": "James owns golf clubs and still shoots 110."
  },
  "inside_right": {
    "signoff": "Happy 50th Dad. Love Sophie and Buster.",
    "qr": "https://roast.pet/r/abc123"
  }
}

Renderer should produce:
- front
- inside spread
- back
- flat preview
- realistic mockup
- Prodigi print files

CHAT PREVIEW UX

First preview shown in Muse should be a realistic product mockup.

Then optionally:
=E2=80=9CView inside=E2=80=9D

which shows the flattened inside spread.

That is enough confidence for most users.

Conversation example:

User:
=E2=80=9CMake Dad a birthday card from Buster.=E2=80=9D

Muse:
[card mockup image]

=E2=80=9CI made Buster a comedy-club birthday card. The QR opens his full r=
oast.=E2=80=9D

User:
=E2=80=9CUse the photo where his ears are sticking up and make the headline=
 meaner.=E2=80=9D

MCP:
replace_image(...)
set_headline(...)
render_preview()

Muse:
[updated mockup image]

User:
=E2=80=9CPerfect.=E2=80=9D

Muse:
=E2=80=9CSend it?=E2=80=9D

User:
=E2=80=9CYes.=E2=80=9D

MCP:
render_print_files()
order_physical()

PROVIDER ABSTRACTION

Do not hard-code Prodigi into the product model.

Use:

fulfil(order, provider=3D"prodigi")

with adapters for:
- Prodigi
- Printify
- Gelato
- any future physical-card provider

Same for editor/render engine:

render(design, engine=3D"fabric")
render(design, engine=3D"polotno")

We should be able to swap the rendering/editor stack next week without
changing the Roast.pet / Muse contract.

THIS IS THE KEY PRINCIPLE

The moat is NOT a custom editor.

The moat is:
- persistent Pog identity
- humour / FunnyLabs context
- reusable performance history
- product templates
- agent-accessible commerce flow
- physical + digital reveal loop

The editor and fulfilment engines are replaceable infrastructure.

CHECKPOINT 1

Take the existing synthetic Etsy fixture and make this real:

input:
- 4 pet photos
- recipient
- gossip
- tone
- signoff

output:
1. canonical Pog identity
2. deterministic card JSON
3. flat preview
4. realistic mockup
5. print-ready card
6. unique QR/reveal
7. FreakTown performance
8. AR/reveal handoff
9. successful physical order

The user should be able to complete the whole thing from Muse via our
MCP, while Etsy remains another acquisition adapter.

Attached is the current Checkpoint-1 demo pack with fake customer
uploads, normalization targets, card/AR/video contracts and QA cases.

=E2=80=94 ChatGPT

